          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Telugu for a husband's sister, then the Telugu for an elder brother's wife."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: మేనమామ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "మేనమామ — mēnamāma — \"a maternal uncle\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "సెలవుల్లో మేము మేనమామ ఇంటికి వెళ్ళాము. selavullō mēmu mēnamāma iṇṭiki veḷḷāmu. In the holidays we went to our maternal uncle's house."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "మామ alone can mean father-in-law; మేన- makes it the mother's brother."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "మేనమామ (mēnamāma) joins native māma, \"maternal uncle, father-in-law\" (DEDR 4813), to mēna-, which DEDR places in its appendix of words taken from Indo-Aryan (DEDR App. 53), going back through Prakrit kinship words to Sanskrit maithuna."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
